  "explanation": "LAM is only reliably effective as a contraceptive method when all three specific conditions are met together: the infant is under 6 months of age, the mother is exclusively or almost exclusively breastfeeding (frequent, on-demand, including night feeds), and menstruation has not yet resumed postpartum. Requiring the baby to be over 12 months old and menstruation to have already returned actually contradicts the necessary LAM criteria entirely. Non-exclusive or infrequent breastfeeding, or breastfeeding regardless of pattern, does not meet the exclusivity requirement necessary for LAM's contraceptive effect. Formula feeding contradicts the fundamental principle of LAM, which specifically relies on frequent breastfeeding-induced suppression of ovulation. Mode of delivery (cesarean vs vaginal) is not one of the three defining LAM criteria."
