  "explanation": "Sick Building Syndrome describes a situation where a significant proportion of building occupants experience acute, often nonspecific symptoms (headache, fatigue, eye/throat irritation, difficulty concentrating) that correlate with time spent in a particular building, without any single, specific, identifiable causative agent, in contrast to 'building-related illness' where a specific cause (like Legionella) can be identified. If a single, specific pathogen is identified as the cause, the condition would more accurately be classified as a 'building-related illness' rather than the specifically nonspecific, no-single-cause definition of Sick Building Syndrome. It refers to occupant health/comfort symptoms, not structural or architectural collapse risk, which would be an entirely different building safety concern. Sick Building Syndrome can affect occupants of various ages present in the building, not exclusively elderly individuals. It refers to a health-related occupant syndrome, not a structural condemnation status determined by regulatory authorities for demolition purposes."
