  "explanation": "Anopheles mosquitoes typically prefer breeding in relatively clean, still or slow-moving freshwater bodies such as ponds, marshes, slow streams, and rainwater collections, distinguishing their breeding preference from some other mosquito species. They are not characteristically associated with heavily polluted, highly organic sewage water as their primary preferred breeding habitat, unlike some other vector species (like certain Culex mosquitoes) that tolerate more polluted water. They are not marine/saltwater breeders; Anopheles mosquitoes require freshwater environments for larval development, not saline water. Fast-flowing rivers with strong currents are generally unsuitable and atypical breeding sites for Anopheles, which prefer calmer, more stable water conditions for larval development. Anopheles mosquitoes breed in various outdoor natural and man-made water collections, not exclusively indoors, since indoor water storage would represent only one of many possible breeding site types."
