  "explanation": "GeneXpert MTB/RIF is now recommended by WHO as an initial rapid diagnostic test for TB, using molecular PCR-based technology to detect Mycobacterium tuberculosis DNA and simultaneously identify rifampicin resistance within about 2 hours, significantly faster and more sensitive than traditional smear microscopy. Sputum smear microscopy, while historically important and still used, has lower sensitivity (especially in paucibacillary or HIV-co-infected cases) and cannot detect drug resistance, making it less preferred as the sole initial test where GeneXpert is available. Chest X-ray alone is a supportive radiological tool but cannot provide microbiological confirmation of active TB or distinguish it definitively from other conditions, so it is not sufficient as a standalone diagnostic method. The tuberculin skin test primarily indicates prior exposure/infection (including latent TB) rather than confirming active disease, and does not provide the rapid, specific diagnostic confirmation of active TB disease with resistance testing. Complete blood count is a nonspecific test that cannot diagnose or rule out TB."
