  "explanation": "The standard WHO-recommended intensive phase for new TB cases uses four drugs: Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (often abbreviated HRZE), given daily for 2 months, followed by a continuation phase typically with Isoniazid and Rifampicin for 4 more months. Streptomycin is an injectable second-line-type agent historically used in certain retreatment regimens, not part of the standard first-line four-drug regimen for new cases (which uses Ethambutol, not Streptomycin, as the fourth drug). Azithromycin and Ceftriaxone are not standard first-line anti-TB drugs at all; they are antibiotics used for other bacterial infections, not part of the TB regimen. Using only two drugs (Isoniazid and Pyrazinamide, or Rifampicin and Ethambutol) during the intensive phase would be inadequate and would significantly increase the risk of treatment failure and drug resistance development, since four-drug combination therapy is specifically required during this phase."
