  "explanation": "Microscopic examination of Giemsa-stained thick and thin peripheral blood smears remains the gold standard for malaria diagnosis, allowing detection of the parasite, species identification, and quantification of parasitemia (parasite density), essential information for both diagnosis and treatment/prognosis. A complete blood count alone may show nonspecific findings (like anemia or thrombocytopenia) suggestive of malaria but cannot specifically identify the parasite or confirm the diagnosis definitively, unlike direct microscopic visualization. Chest X-ray has no role in malaria diagnosis, since malaria is a systemic parasitic infection affecting red blood cells, not primarily a respiratory/pulmonary disease requiring radiological diagnosis. Urine dipstick testing has no established role in diagnosing malaria; it is used for entirely different purposes (like detecting proteinuria, glucose, or urinary tract infection markers). Stool microscopy is used for diagnosing various intestinal parasitic infections (like helminths or protozoa affecting the GI tract), not for diagnosing malaria, which is a blood-borne parasitic infection requiring blood examination."
