  "explanation": "Once clinical symptoms of rabies appear, the disease is essentially universally fatal, with virtually no reported survivors historically without highly experimental and rarely successful interventions, making this one of the most important reasons why prompt post-exposure prophylaxis (wound care, rabies immunoglobulin, and vaccine series) BEFORE symptom onset is an absolute medical emergency and critical public health priority. It is a serious misconception to suggest 'complete spontaneous recovery' occurs in nearly all symptomatic cases; the well-established, tragic reality is the opposite, with near-universal fatality once symptoms manifest, underscoring the critical importance of pre-symptomatic prophylaxis. Rabies is emphatically NOT a mild, self-limiting illness; it is one of the most lethal infectious diseases known once clinical symptoms develop, making this a dangerous and incorrect characterization. Rabies is caused by a VIRUS, not a bacterium, so antibiotics have no efficacy against it whatsoever, and there is no simple effective cure once symptoms begin; this represents both an incorrect etiological understanding and a false claim of treatability. Bite wound SIZE is not the determining factor for rabies risk; the RISK depends on factors like the biting animal's rabies status, the bite location (proximity to CNS), and whether appropriate post-exposure prophylaxis is administered, not simply wound size, and no bite from a potentially rabid animal should be dismissed as posing 'no risk at all.'"
