  "explanation": "The IHR (2005) framework aims to enable rapid international coordination and response to serious public health events with potential for international spread, while balancing this with minimizing unnecessary interference with international trade and travel, reflecting a framework designed for proportionate, evidence-based response rather than blanket restrictive measures. It does not call for restricting all international travel permanently regardless of actual risk assessment; the framework specifically emphasizes PROPORTIONATE responses based on actual risk, explicitly aiming to avoid unnecessary trade/travel disruption. The IHR framework is NOT limited to a single specific named disease; it is a broad, flexible framework designed to apply to ANY public health event that could potentially constitute a PHEIC, regardless of the specific pathogen or cause involved. Rather than eliminating national surveillance systems, the IHR framework specifically REQUIRES and relies upon robust national-level surveillance and reporting capacity as an essential foundation for the international notification system to function effectively. The IHR framework applies to ALL WHO member states, not exclusively high-income countries; it establishes obligations and rights applicable broadly across the international community, not selectively based on income level."
