  "explanation": "According to the Spaulding classification, items that contact only intact skin (like blood pressure cuffs, stethoscopes) are classified as 'noncritical' items, for which low-level disinfection or simple cleaning is generally considered adequate, since intact skin provides an effective barrier against most microorganisms, unlike items contacting sterile tissue or mucous membranes. Requiring full sterilization for all such noncritical, intact-skin-contact items would be an unnecessary, resource-inefficient overapplication of infection control principles, which are specifically graduated based on the item's intended use and associated infection risk level. Complete absence of any cleaning/disinfection would be inappropriate and could allow accumulation of pathogens on shared equipment surfaces, even if the risk is lower than for more invasive device categories. High-level disinfection (as required for 'semicritical' items contacting mucous membranes) would be an excessive and unnecessary standard for simple noncritical, intact-skin-contact items like blood pressure cuffs. The Spaulding classification framework does not mandate banning reusable items in favor of exclusively single-use disposables; reusable noncritical items are entirely appropriate when properly cleaned/disinfected according to their specific risk classification."
