  "explanation": "Hypertension diagnosis traditionally requires consistently elevated readings of systolic BP ≥140 mmHg and/or diastolic BP ≥90 mmHg on separate occasions/visits (some newer guidelines use lower thresholds like 130/80, but 140/90 remains a widely referenced traditional threshold in many exam contexts), reflecting the importance of confirmed, sustained elevation rather than a single isolated reading. A systolic BP below 90 mmHg would actually represent hypotension (low blood pressure), the opposite condition, not hypertension. Diagnosis specifically requires CONSISTENT elevated readings on separate occasions, not merely a single isolated elevated reading, since blood pressure can be transiently elevated due to anxiety, activity, or measurement technique issues (white coat effect), necessitating confirmation. A diastolic BP below 60 mmHg would be considered low, not elevated, and is not consistent with a hypertension diagnosis. A blood pressure of exactly 120/80 mmHg represents a normal (or in some classifications 'elevated/pre-hypertensive' borderline) reading, not diagnostic of hypertension by standard threshold criteria."
