  "explanation": "Effective NCD prevention requires genuine multisectoral collaboration, recognizing that key NCD risk factors (tobacco use, unhealthy diet, physical inactivity, alcohol use) are significantly influenced by policies and structures across many sectors beyond health alone, including finance/taxation (tobacco/sugar taxes), agriculture (food production systems), education (health literacy), urban planning (spaces for physical activity), and trade policy, requiring coordinated action across all these domains for meaningful population-level impact. Restricting involvement to ONLY the Ministry of Health would directly contradict the fundamental multisectoral principle, since many of the most powerful NCD prevention levers (like taxation policy or urban planning) lie outside the traditional health sector's direct authority. While individual behavior does play a role in NCD risk, framing prevention as 'solely the responsibility of individual patients, with no government/sectoral role' ignores the well-established importance of societal, structural, and policy-level determinants that shape the environments in which individuals make health-related choices. While international organizations (like WHO) provide important guidance and support, effective NCD control fundamentally requires NATIONAL government leadership and multisectoral coordination within each country, not an approach that excludes national government responsibility entirely. Multisectoral approaches are, in fact, widely regarded by global health authorities as ESSENTIAL and evidence-supported for effective NCD prevention, not something that has been 'proven ineffective,' making this option directly contrary to established public health consensus."
