  "explanation": "The 1978 Alma-Ata Declaration formally articulated Primary Health Care as the central strategy for achieving the goal of Health for All, emphasizing accessible, community-based, essential healthcare as the foundation of national health systems, representing a landmark shift in global health policy thinking. WHO itself was established earlier, in 1948, not created by the Alma-Ata Declaration, which came three decades later and addressed strategic approach, not organizational founding. Vaccination as a medical practice long predates 1978, with smallpox vaccination pioneered by Edward Jenner in the late 18th century; the Declaration did not introduce this concept for the first time in history. The Alma-Ata Declaration specifically emphasized primary, not tertiary hospital-based, care as the foundational strategy, representing a deliberate shift in emphasis toward accessible community-level care rather than a focus on high-level specialized institutions. While comprehensive PHC encompasses various health needs, the Declaration was not narrowly or solely concerned with mental health services; it addressed a broad, comprehensive vision of primary healthcare covering multiple essential health needs and services."
