  "explanation": "The WHO health systems framework identifies six core interconnected building blocks: service delivery, health workforce, health information systems, access to essential medicines and technologies, health financing, and leadership/governance, together forming the comprehensive structure needed for a functioning, resilient health system capable of improving health outcomes. Reducing health systems to only hospitals and clinics vastly oversimplifies this comprehensive, multi-component framework, which recognizes that infrastructure is just one of six essential interconnected building blocks, not the entirety of what constitutes a functioning system. While health workforce, including doctor density, is indeed one recognized building block, focusing only on doctors per capita ignores the other five essential components that together determine overall system function. Pharmaceutical production capacity might relate tangentially to the access to medicines building block, but this narrow framing omits the five other essential, distinctly recognized components of the comprehensive WHO framework. Patient satisfaction is one possible outcome or quality indicator that might be tracked within health information systems, but it is not itself one of the six formally recognized building blocks of the WHO health systems framework."
