  "explanation": "Life expectancy at birth serves as a valuable summary health indicator precisely because it reflects the cumulative impact of numerous factors affecting mortality risk across the entire population and lifespan, including but not limited to healthcare access and quality, nutritional status, sanitation and environmental conditions, and broader socioeconomic determinants of health, making it a useful though not perfectly specific overall population health status indicator. It is not accurate to say it reflects only healthcare system quality with no influence from any other factor; in fact, its value as an indicator comes precisely from capturing this broader range of influences beyond healthcare alone, including social determinants. The claim of no relationship to any health system or social factors is directly contrary to the well-established understanding of life expectancy as being substantially influenced by exactly these health system and social factors, which is the entire basis for its use as a summary health indicator. Life expectancy at birth is calculated using comprehensive age-specific mortality data across all age groups using life table methodology, not calculated using only data from people over age 65, which would be an inaccurate and incomplete description of the actual calculation methodology. Life expectancy at birth is a general population health indicator reflecting overall mortality patterns across the lifespan, not a measure specifically of maternal mortality, which is a distinctly different, more specific indicator tracking a different aspect of population health."
