  "explanation": "High reliance on out-of-pocket payment is recognized as problematic because it can create significant financial barriers preventing people, especially those with lower incomes, from accessing needed care, and can push households into poverty through catastrophic health expenditures when serious illness strikes, representing a major concern addressed by Universal Health Coverage and health financing reform efforts globally. The well-documented, substantial negative impact of high out-of-pocket expenditure on healthcare access and equity directly contradicts any claim of no impact whatsoever. Out-of-pocket financing is generally considered one of the least efficient and least equitable ways to finance healthcare at a population level compared to prepayment/pooling mechanisms like insurance or tax-based financing, not the most efficient and fair way, which misrepresents established health financing evidence and policy consensus. Rather than eliminating any need for health insurance systems, the well-recognized problems associated with high out-of-pocket spending are precisely why health insurance and other prepayment/risk-pooling mechanisms are considered important complementary or alternative financing approaches to reduce this burden. The burden of out-of-pocket health expenditure disproportionately and severely affects lower-income households, who have less capacity to absorb unexpected health costs, not something that only affects wealthy individuals."
