  "explanation": "Health literacy refers to an individual's capacity to obtain, process, and understand basic health information and services necessary to make appropriate, informed health decisions, representing an important determinant of health outcomes and effective use of healthcare services, relevant to the general public regardless of formal medical training. Health literacy is a much broader, more practically relevant concept than narrowly being able to read complex medical textbooks fluently; it centers on functional understanding needed for everyday health decisions, not academic medical literature comprehension. While multilingual ability could be one component contributing to communication in some contexts, health literacy is fundamentally about UNDERSTANDING AND USING health information effectively, not simply about language fluency in multiple languages without relation to health understanding specifically. Health literacy is a concept relevant to the GENERAL PUBLIC broadly, explicitly not requiring or relating to completion of a medical degree; it concerns the everyday health information needs and decision-making capacity of ordinary individuals, not medical professional training. Health literacy concerns a person's understanding and use of health information and services, having no direct definitional relationship to their financial capacity to afford private health insurance, which is an entirely separate economic/access consideration."
