        "topic": "Skin Pathology",
        "difficulty": "Harder",
        "source": "Robbins",
        "question": "A 50-year-old female presents with flaccid, easily ruptured blisters on her skin and severe oral mucosal ulcerations. When the physician rubs normal-appearing skin, the upper epidermis easily shears off (positive Nikolsky sign). Immunofluorescence of a skin biopsy shows a 'net-like' (reticular) pattern of IgG deposition. What is the specific molecular target of these autoantibodies?",
