        "topic": "Skin Pathology",
        "difficulty": "Normal",
        "source": "Robbins",
        "question": "A 60-year-old male presents with diffuse, scaly, erythematous plaques on his trunk that he assumed was eczema for years. However, a recent skin biopsy reveals a dense dermal infiltrate of atypical CD4+ T-cells with highly characteristic 'cerebriform' (brain-like) nuclei. These cells are invading the epidermis to form Pautrier microabscesses. Diagnosis?",
