        "correct": 3,
        "exp": "Allergic Contact Dermatitis (e.g., to nickel, poison ivy, or neomycin) is a classic Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity reaction. Langerhans cells in the skin process the antigen and present it to naive T-cells in the lymph nodes. Upon re-exposure, these primed Th1 cells release cytokines that recruit macrophages, causing severe tissue damage and blistering 24-72 hours later.",
